the protestant reformation was to put it  bluntly not well received by the  catholic church it wasn't long after  people like martin luther and john  calvin launched their attacks against  the church that western europe was  bitterly divided over what the correct  christian practices were but in the east  sat the orthodox christian world created  from a previous falling out with rome  who must have had their own opinions of  what was going on so what were those  opinions how did the orthodox world  react to the protestant reformation so  as of martin luther's theses the primary  centre of orthodoxy was constantinople  which was under the ottoman empire and  itself oversaw two other major orthodox  centers kiev under the polish lithuanian  commonwealth and moscow run by the  moskovites and to all of them their  reaction was largely negative with some  caveats starting with constantinople  because the patriarch there was the  highest ranking person in the orthodox  world the reaction was largely one of  concern there was no love for rome in  the east but the reformation wasn't  initially about founding a new church  but about reforming the existing one  which to those in the east just meant  that they were still catholics just once  getting it all even more wrong which  meant any christian unity would be much  harder and also these heresies had a  tendency of spreading which was not  something they wanted in their own lands  but barring these initial thoughts there  wasn't much in the way of formal  communication between orthodox and  protestant christians for many decades  whilst the theological realms didn't at  first interact all too much this didn't  stop the political ones from doing so  and in moscow sar ivan iv better known  as ivan the best dad ever had both a  severe and optimistic response to  protestantism for him this heresy  weakened his catholic neighbours and  previously such schisms had led to civil  war meaning that for moscow it presented  an opportunity for westward expansion  personally though the tenets of  protestantism disgusted him and his  primary concern was these ideas  spreading into his realm the same way  that they had done in prussia and poland  lithuania and guess what they did  leading to several high-profile  prosecutions for what was then known as  frivolous thinking so back to church's  talking there was some optimism in the  late 16th century that the orthodox and  lutheran church could cooperate and in  the eyes of some even reunite and the  first step in this was seeing if the  doctrines were compatible and this was  done by sending the translated augsburg  confession to the orthodox church in  1575.  this was a list of 21 fundamental  pillars of the protestant faith sent to  the patriarch of constantinople and he  was not pleased with them he responded  that seven of these creeds were outright  heretical and so good luck in hell and  whilst the orthodox church agreed with  the rest of the articles lutherans were  doing them wrong at first the orthodox  church tried to push for cooperation  with the protestants but it wasn't  shocking to know that both sides were  pretty firm in their beliefs and despite  more communication by the end of the  16th century both denominations had  accepted that there would be no unity  between them and so the orthodox  reaction to that was the same as it was  for catholics they were just heretics  and for the protestants the feeling was  mutual  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons james bissonette  kelly moneymaker mr wolf sky chappelle  jerry lamdin yn hockey marvin castle  jordan longley gareth turner rod d  martin bugley wiggily captain cydog  spencer lightfoot corso wolfe gustav  swan matthew shipley winston caywood  robert wetzel aaron the white marcus  arsenal maggie paxkowski anthony beckett  alex schwinn moe the mukwopa coppertone  spinning three plates ben ivinson  charles the first adam stalter and  scottish trekkie
 

